            1. Why Basement Waterproofing Is a Checklist, Not One Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most expensive mistake we see homeowners make isn't buying the wrong brand — it's assuming basement waterproofing is a single purchase. A gallon of masonry coating and a pump aren't interchangeable; they solve completely different problems. Coating stops moisture from wicking through porous concrete. Hydraulic cement plugs an active hole or gap. A crack injection kit permanently seals one specific crack. A sump pump removes water that's already reached the floor. A dehumidifier manages ambient humidity in the air. None of these substitutes for the others, and buying only one when your basement actually needs two or three is why so many DIY waterproofing attempts fail within a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide treats basement waterproofing the way a contractor's supply list actually looks: a set of distinct tools, each assigned to a specific job. Some basements need every item on this list; many need only two or three, and buying the other five would just be money spent on shelf space. The <a href="#which-supplies">section below</a> walks through how to match your specific symptoms to the right supplies, so you're not guessing, overbuying, or coming home from the hardware store with the wrong tool for the job in front of you.
          </p>

