            2. Diagnose Before You Shop
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you add anything to a cart, spend twenty minutes figuring out exactly what your basement is doing, because the wrong supply for your specific problem is money and a weekend wasted. Start with the <strong>foil test</strong>: tape a 12x12-inch square of aluminum foil tightly to a problem wall and check it after 24-48 hours. Moisture on the room-facing side of the foil means condensation, which points toward a dehumidifier rather than a wall coating. Moisture on the wall-facing side means water is genuinely migrating through the foundation, and you're shopping for a coating, cement, or injection kit instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            From there, walk the basement during or right after a heavy rain if you can, since active water problems are far easier to diagnose while they're happening than from dried evidence a day later. Note exactly where water appears: at the base of the wall along the floor-wall joint usually points to hydrostatic pressure pushing water up from below, which a coating alone won't fix. A single damp patch high on a poured wall, especially one that traces a vertical line, usually means a shrinkage crack that's a candidate for injection. A hole around a pipe penetration or a honeycombed patch of rough concrete that's actively seeping calls for hydraulic cement first, before you do anything else. And a general clammy, musty feeling with no visible wet spot anywhere often means your real problem is ambient humidity, not water intrusion at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also rule out the free causes before buying anything. Clogged gutters, downspouts that dump within a few feet of the foundation, and soil that has settled to slope toward the house instead of away from it are responsible for a surprising share of "waterproofing emergencies." None of the eight supplies below can fully compensate for a downspout pouring a roof's worth of water against one corner of your foundation during every storm, so fix the exterior basics first — it costs nothing and it might turn out to be the whole fix.
          </p>
        </div>

