        <!-- SECTION 6: Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Common Mistakes When Buying Basement Waterproofing Supplies
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most common mistake is buying only one item from this list and expecting it to solve a problem it was never designed for — coating a wall that's actually flooding from hydrostatic pressure, or running a dehumidifier in a basement with an active leak. Every product on this page does its job well within its lane and does nothing outside it, and no amount of spending on the wrong supply will fix a problem it wasn't built to address. Diagnose first, using the process above, and buy specifically for what you find.
          </p>

          <p>
            Undersizing the sump pump is the second-most expensive mistake we see, and it's an easy one to make because pumps are often sold by horsepower alone without context for what that actually moves. A 1/3 HP pump might be fine for a small, shallow pit with modest inflow, but the same pump can be overwhelmed in a deeper pit or a home with a genuinely high water table, cycling constantly and burning out prematurely. When in doubt, size up rather than down — a pump that runs less often lasts longer than one working at its limit during every storm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skipping the battery backup to save $200-300 is a false economy that shows up exactly when you can least afford it: during the storm that also knocks out your power. We'd rather see a homeowner buy a mid-tier primary pump with a backup than a premium primary pump with no backup at all, because the backup is what closes the single biggest vulnerability in any sump-pump-based setup. Similarly, buying a water leak sensor and then never testing its battery, or letting a sump pump's backup battery sit untested for years, defeats the purpose of owning either — put a reminder on your calendar to test both every six months, ideally right before your region's rainy season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, don't buy hydraulic cement or a crack injection kit and let it sit in a garage for years before using it. Hydraulic cement in particular has a shelf life once the bag has been opened to humid air, and a kit's epoxy paste and resin cartridges can degrade over time even sealed. If you're buying these proactively rather than for an immediate repair, check the manufacturer's stated shelf life and plan to use or replace them within that window rather than assuming a five-year-old bag in the corner will perform the same as a fresh one.
          </p>
        </div>

