            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What supplies do I need to waterproof a basement myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical DIY basement waterproofing project, plan on: a masonry waterproofing coating (like DRYLOK Extreme) for damp walls, hydraulic cement for any active leaks or holes, a polyurethane crack injection kit for a single weeping crack in a poured wall, a reliable primary sump pump if you have a sump pit, a battery backup sump pump for storm power outages, a water leak sensor for early warning, a dehumidifier for ambient humidity, and 6 mil plastic sheeting as a vapor barrier if you're finishing the floor. Which of these you actually need depends on diagnosing your specific water problem first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most important basement waterproofing supply to buy first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your specific problem, but if you have a sump pit, a reliable primary sump pump with a battery backup is the highest-priority purchase, since pump failure during a storm is the leading cause of catastrophic basement flooding. If your basement only has damp, musty walls with no standing water, a masonry waterproofing coating like DRYLOK Extreme is the right first purchase instead. Diagnose the water source before buying — the wrong supply for your specific problem won't fix it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need hydraulic cement and a crack injection kit, or just one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They solve different problems. Hydraulic cement is for stopping active, running water through a hole or gap right now — it sets in minutes, even underwater, but cures rigid and can crack again under seasonal foundation movement. A polyurethane crack injection kit is for permanently sealing a specific weeping crack in a poured-concrete wall with a flexible material that moves with the foundation. Many DIY projects use both: hydraulic cement to stop an emergency leak immediately, followed by a proper crack injection repair once the area is stabilized.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dehumidifier a substitute for basement waterproofing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A dehumidifier manages ambient humidity in the air — it does nothing to stop water intrusion through walls, floors, or cracks. Running a dehumidifier in a basement with an active leak treats a symptom while ignoring the cause, and the moisture load from a real leak will typically overwhelm what any dehumidifier can remove. Use a dehumidifier to address the musty smell and lingering humidity that remains after the actual water source has been fixed with a coating, injection kit, or drainage system.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a basement waterproofing supplies checklist cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A full DIY supplies checklist typically runs $600-1,200: roughly $45-150 for a gallon or two of masonry coating, $10-20 for hydraulic cement, $60-120 for a crack injection kit, $150-250 for a quality primary sump pump, $200-350 for a battery backup system, $15-20 per water leak sensor, $200-300 for a basement-rated dehumidifier, and $30-60 for a roll of 6 mil vapor barrier sheeting. Actual cost depends on how many of these categories your specific basement needs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can these supplies fix a basement that's already flooding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A sump pump can actively remove water that's already reached the floor, and hydraulic cement can plug an actively running leak, but none of these individual supplies is a substitute for a full interior drainage system if your basement is experiencing serious, recurring hydrostatic-pressure flooding. In that case, these supplies are the right complement to — not replacement for — a professionally installed interior perimeter drain. See our Best Basement Waterproofing Systems guide for the full comparison of drainage-level solutions.
              </p>
            </div>
